#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "6060e2fe-05b3-587d-b5dd-3cdab705eb05" :question/math-academy-id "e-21546" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) =\nA\n\\mathbf{x}(t) +\n\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t),$ where $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for $\\mathbf x_p(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}'_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t) = A \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi' = A \\Phi$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\Phi^{-1}(t) \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L8}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that ![](images/e2/e2e16ba2ee71f82214649c533dc658cdba72e3b3f49cf8563f117ee2fe89a07d.svg) matrix.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by  ![](images/2d/2dd0b78f14b4f044b6c747da364728bb5555f5c36710b6bf66ab74cc7f5e294f.svg) both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into ![](images/01/01ab649cd1dec87ec0dd5c1f3f8f79ce76553d52463ae2c6102c0ce7be67163d.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) =A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $\\mathbf{c}(t)$ of $\\Phi$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}'(t) =A\\mathbf{c}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}'(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $\\Phi.$ As a result, this means that $\\Phi' = A \\Phi.$ Therefore, $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that ![](images/d5/d59c4945960aa127fa7c03bcf32bd9bab88f237932bf222dd796a3153145ed4c.svg) matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}.$ Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $\\Phi$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by ![](images/13/13d7995b324919ea6f2f7b3cb45abc58bb48c87d82165aea6bb455059952764c.svg) both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1},$ $\\textrm{L7},$ and $\\textrm{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\textrm{L7}$ into $\\textrm{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into  ![](images/28/28362ce6a7bd47f8c810d5fc3772428094f65e66a4e88df145b0c91a9e9016e9.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "1d203940-c05d-5f30-a8ad-c618d6d6ba5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21547" :question/problem "![](images/02/02f22b5daa1290af87e8a15340bd2e75abdb9305136f9b7f3162763e4dee440e.svg)\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute $\\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t)$ and the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ of the system." :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n![](images/31/3139ce9adc8c4803bc8cf3332e76c2bad4cb35489024478178ab74023dee7e44.svg)\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-2{e}^{-4t})-2{e}^{-4t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-4t} & -2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-4t} & -2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & -2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{t} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "ce663953-3e5e-55e2-a8db-6d7a531496cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21548" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a4a67e816a9de4c0278f607d2e98ceae6cbd315df086980d7a604da88ed1926.svg)\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"images/07/07ad5e11661f5d8ede3728e39999890e71848ffc0290b471e9b4130f026431a2.svg\"/></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\boxed{\\rule{0pt}{1.3em}\\phantom{xxxxxxxx}}$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n![](images/85/8570eb051b14077d86cbe0cda29f79d12de8529001c6e9b3a2698c107792ef1b.svg)\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n![](images/9f/9fe602a563fbfbfd3f3331df7243ebf1aa5aa1c90a01550a033d4a4dfd0f0639.svg)\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}-0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 0\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=2t$ and $\\text{d}v=e^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}-\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, the integrand is zero, so the integral is zero.\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(2t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112cd61c-d6ec-5b21-a43e-abee24d650f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302569} {:answer/id #uuid "12334453-77d2-5dd2-99d3-4f6f14bdfae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302580} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7e4243-0a26-5991-9f01-96b7ce162b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303429} {:answer/id #uuid "c40013df-d378-5245-93a2-10d8db23f392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c7e4243-0a26-5991-9f01-96b7ce162b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303429} :answer-field/id #uuid "13afdc2d-6b3b-5524-8261-72ad27b914fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45f36c65-eecf-50e8-8dca-3548b95a781e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is an invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302899} {:answer/id #uuid "67264ce4-9890-51a9-9cc8-0bd8628bdbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is the identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303081} {:answer/id #uuid "8682a994-8464-5273-a469-d10cd58209c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303295} {:answer/id #uuid "89cf5667-29c6-517a-be74-ef409b12293c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8682a994-8464-5273-a469-d10cd58209c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303295} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ad3cfbc-b21d-58d3-bad3-220676ab157d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304363} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28361b8e-e41a-5147-9650-48cd1c63fe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302716} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8d0101-c035-5b9a-8f40-3244438c6f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303508} {:answer/id #uuid "d5fe574b-0131-545e-9eb4-d166f8e72cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303782} {:answer/id #uuid "efb20cea-cfc2-5903-af87-5bbc8f3a74a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5fe574b-0131-545e-9eb4-d166f8e72cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303782} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4012936-5a67-5c5b-9007-eb2b1e0f5daa" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ad3fcdc-246d-580f-b053-3062ecfa179a" :question/math-academy-id "q-330877" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eabf843f-76af-5374-87f3-015cd7c21177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eabf843f-76af-5374-87f3-015cd7c21177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365600} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b0824a7-3977-53c2-8ae9-fab24fac62a7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365599} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7d357d-8554-535e-9625-2f454e5a740e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365602} {:answer/id #uuid "2014d6d7-4139-5c85-9128-fa0bef9f20e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2014d6d7-4139-5c85-9128-fa0bef9f20e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482873} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2490d5f-d96a-5160-b6f7-a7d11c4a92d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a01f2ab9-e122-54f4-be8c-8d60cf41b11c" :question/math-academy-id "q-330893" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-10{e}^{-2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{4t}-(-3{e}^{4t})\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-6t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -10{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cf97b47-a716-5db4-a736-9116713e53e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483001} {:answer/id #uuid "b732aa44-b145-5c84-9ef0-81ea7fa0094b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is the identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483123} {:answer/id #uuid "db14b3e6-8255-5b15-af0a-c43b9ec9f0b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is an invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483181} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a583d8-d211-5fa0-9f95-1776becf6650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cf97b47-a716-5db4-a736-9116713e53e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483001} :answer-field/id #uuid "345ab8ae-cd66-52b2-9a96-cf0b08ce6895" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e47be19-7850-5185-a8be-4f097e3d06a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482918} {:answer/id #uuid "872cda15-912a-58ab-bcc0-20f624fd722f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483047} {:answer/id #uuid "dd301c04-28fc-586b-bd66-53538190b1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483184} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cb65ee-c4ba-5892-87e8-7031e44aace7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd301c04-28fc-586b-bd66-53538190b1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483184} :answer-field/id #uuid "686d35a7-2873-597a-8c04-798dd6392868" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb798f6-eb02-5eb0-b90e-3818bf565735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482886} {:answer/id #uuid "43cbc5c9-67b9-5e07-8eb7-2a5e0e21e482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482928} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9893e8-8c90-5213-9bbf-617a8a90550e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483079} {:answer/id #uuid "a38f4d63-059b-5216-94a8-f8f05c6278e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cb798f6-eb02-5eb0-b90e-3818bf565735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482886} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0979245-762e-5229-aacd-89b1d72de194" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc370c50-c95d-53af-9285-c137ac273145" :question/math-academy-id "q-336704" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-2}} by substitution.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into {{field-3}} ." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Let's substitute ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$ from $\\text{L4}$ into $\\text{L3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+\\cancel{AΦu(t)}=\\cancel{AΦu(t)}+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3}\\text{ and }\\text{L4}$ by substitution.\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into  $\\text{L1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3815ccb0-0a02-5fd6-a641-0b543d3401e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482908} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8d746b-dfe9-5dcc-be99-d1e679811b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482975} {:answer/id #uuid "c559ce39-ac3e-51a2-87df-019730cfd49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483141} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3d2e76-22e3-519f-9402-f852a4dd6ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id 