align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2{e}^{3t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{-19}{6}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-{e}^{-2t})-{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 2{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{6}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "849dc5ee-b92a-5d12-862e-038289763703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309779} {:answer/id #uuid "69172624-3326-54b0-b3c7-c380d2de2937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{5}e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69172624-3326-54b0-b3c7-c380d2de2937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{5}e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482994} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2ec8789-f8e2-5bcb-a05b-12d156ee8c7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f9f8a0-0df1-5b62-8c16-587f7c825e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64f9f8a0-0df1-5b62-8c16-587f7c825e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309527} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccc76a66-e26b-5e7b-8d53-6b7b589d9a0c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a91efb90-d803-5dba-81eb-b92f394b29e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-337281" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-4{e}^{-3t})-5{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{t} \\\\ -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2aca50a-3aaf-509c-a35b-f9a9fcddc26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}e^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2aca50a-3aaf-509c-a35b-f9a9fcddc26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}e^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483220} :answer-field/id #uuid "898f9d60-0210-5c29-be2c-83afc576e947" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483292} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fec73258-a6f9-59da-abbe-d2238c0a8b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fec73258-a6f9-59da-abbe-d2238c0a8b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483240} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc5b84e5-e81f-590d-82f4-59edebfa2113" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7f6c605-b7b1-59c8-8433-ca15cc2ba91a" :question/math-academy-id "q-337282" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t} & -{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{9}{5}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t} & -{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-{e}^{2t})-3{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15}{e}^{5t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t} & -{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15}{e}^{5t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6d86871-d01c-5231-92db-3b8f225214a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d86871-d01c-5231-92db-3b8f225214a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310711} :answer-field/id #uuid "61d6c572-063a-56d9-9057-94f1a95d8083" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc7807df-c4d5-5658-9ef2-4f270abd2dc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-337286" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$23-15t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot 3{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-4t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}(-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{t}+\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{t}(1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4(3t-1)+9(1-t) \\\\ 4(3t-1)+27(1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3t+5 \\\\ 23-15t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8039f17-10a2-5a2d-8787-b1e6d3df2d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33t-37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8039f17-10a2-5a2d-8787-b1e6d3df2d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33t-37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483096} :answer-field/id #uuid "9324a6eb-07ab-5ed4-84d0-646338f3a965" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "858d57fe-416e-56c5-af8b-4ea6e4298062" :question/math-academy-id "q-337289" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3t-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(4t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =4t{e}^{t}-4{e}^{t} \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =t\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}36(t-1)-(3t+1) \\\\ -(3t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}33t-37 \\\\ -3t-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233241c0-123a-55a0-9903-366c8ec5a21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233241c0-123a-55a0-9903-366c8ec5a21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309015} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a284f86-7e4b-51fd-b771-60a6ffad1c2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7ef0fb5-75be-526d-82d2-8faa19bc8d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-337306" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$39t+23$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot 3{e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\cdot (-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}-\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4(3t-1)+9(t+1) \\\\ 4(3t-1)+27(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}21t+5 \\\\ 39t+23\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65022199-6a0a-59fa-b71e-95b9b1843201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65022199-6a0a-59fa-b71e-95b9b1843201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309528} :answer-field/id #uuid "92650cbf-bf86-5f3b-a335-e3e58d6d03f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eaf12e35-a3fc-5fd0-814c-953ea7b38802" :question/math-academy-id "q-337307" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$30t+20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{6}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{1}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}5(3t-1)+45(t+1) \\\\ 5(3t-1)+9(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}30t+20 \\\\ 12t+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
